    "title": "Data-free reverse engineering of triggers using the winning Trojan ticket yields substantially improved target-label detection and recovered-trigger attack success",
    "content": "Using the winning Trojan ticket as the victim model in a trigger-recovery optimization\n      $$\n      \\min_{z\\in\\{0,1\\}^n,\\;\\delta}\\; \\mathbb{E}_x\\big[\\ell_{\\mathrm{atk}}(x'(z,\\delta);\\phi,t)\\big] + \\gamma\\,h(z,\\delta),\n      $$\n      with $x'(z,\\delta)=(1-z)\\odot x + z\\odot\\delta$, C\\&W targeted loss $\\ell_{\\mathrm{atk}}$, and the regularizer $h$ from prior work, and solving the label-wise optimizations followed by selecting the label with minimum $\\|z^*\\odot\\delta^*\\|_1$ recovers (i) the correct Trojan target label more reliably than applying the same optimization to the dense model and (ii) a recovered trigger that, when stamped onto test images, achieves substantially higher ASR. Representative quantitative results: on ResNet-20s, CIFAR-10, (a) gray-scale trigger: recovered ASR = 91.2% (winning ticket) vs 71.4% (dense baseline); (b) RGB trigger: 99.6% vs 48.0%; (c) clean-label trigger: 99.8% vs 9.6% (an over 90%-point ASR improvement reported). The recovered triggers from winning Trojan tickets are effective even when the recovery optimization uses only 100 Gaussian noise images as base inputs (i.e., \"for free\") rather than any clean images.",
